                                        "sense_id": "41b1ae17-56bd-3180-bd55-2ae62ccd160b",
                                        "entry_id": "acc9fbc7-c089-357f-afb1-c14f067a49d8",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The process whereby the ideas of another are unconsciously incorporated into one's own psyche."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c8477a82-03e9-4cca-8009-b65a6efa3957",
                                                "sense_id": "41b1ae17-56bd-3180-bd55-2ae62ccd160b",
                                                "text": "Sometimes the attempt was made to reduce the inner to the outer world (Condillac, Mach, Avenarius, materialism); sometimes the outer to the inner world (Descartes, Berkeley, Fichte); sometimes the sphere of the absolute to the others (e.g., by trying to infer causally the essence and existence of something divine in general); […]; sometimes the general differentiation of subject and object to pregivenness of the co- or 'fellow-man', to whom an environmental element—as, for instance, 'this tree' — is supposed to be introjected, followed by subsequent introjection by the observer to himself (Avenarius); sometimes one's own body to a merely associative coordination of the self-perception of the own self and organ sensations with the own body as perceived from outside."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
